## How ingress works

Every production inbound message follows the same pipeline:

```
Webhook → verify HMAC → resolve ChannelConnection → persistInboundIfNew → enqueueAgentTurn → Inngest runAgentTurn
```

### Webhook routes

| Route | Provider |
|---|---|
| `POST /api/webhooks/zernio` | Zernio WhatsApp/Instagram |
| `POST /api/webhooks/meta` | HookMyApp → Meta |
| `POST /api/webhooks/hookmyapp/connected` | Channel connected notification |

### `persistInboundIfNew()` (`conversations.ts`)

- **Idempotent** on `providerMessageId` (webhook retries are safe)
- Upserts `Lead` by `(tenantId, channelId, externalUserId)`
- Creates `Conversation` if none open; sets `flowState = agent.flow.start`
- Inserts inbound `Message`

### Inngest enqueue

```58:72:src/lib/flow/run-turn.ts
export async function enqueueAgentTurn(opts: {
  tenantId: string;
  conversationId: string;
  triggerMessageId: string;
  resume?: boolean;
}) {
  await inngest.send({
    name: "agent/turn.requested",
    id: `turn-${opts.triggerMessageId}`,
    data: {
      tenantId: opts.tenantId,
      conversationId: opts.conversationId,
      resume: opts.resume,
    },
  });
}
```

**Why not call the LLM in the webhook?** Meta retries slow handlers; you risk duplicate sends. Fast ACK + durable async job is the contract.

### Demo bypass

`POST /api/demo/message` calls `runTurnNow()` **in-process** (same interpreter, no Inngest) so the UI can wait for a reply.

### Concurrency

```15:20:src/inngest/functions.ts
export const runAgentTurn = inngest.createFunction(
  {
    id: "run-agent-turn",
    retries: 3,
    concurrency: [{ key: "event.data.conversationId", limit: 1 }],
  },
```

One in-flight turn per conversation prevents double-replies when a lead sends two messages quickly.

